# The realtime cadence, and what a refusal does to it.
#
# The other fixtures hold their clock still. This one lets it move, which is the
# only way to see the app decide anything about time: when to ask the feed
# again, how far to back off after a refusal, and what to keep on screen while
# the feed is down.
#
# The script names the answers and never the moments. `feed` lines are a queue,
# taken in order whenever the app's own policy says an attempt is due. A port
# that polls twice as often runs out of them; one that never polls leaves them
# unused. Either way the frames say so, and neither is compared against a
# timetable this file invented.
cache ../cache.db
date 2026-09-11
time 08:00
offset -04:00
size 100 14

# Answer, refuse, refuse, answer.
feed ok rt.json
feed fail http status: 503
feed fail http status: 503
feed ok rt-later.json
# Three more, for the long wait below.
feed ok rt-later.json
feed ok rt-later.json
feed ok rt-later.json
# A refusal after a success, which nothing else here reaches. A backoff that
# survived its own recovery would double from the old value instead of starting
# again at a minute, and only a second failure can show that.
feed fail http status: 503

# To the board at pole 1869, where all three of those trips call.
type 1869
enter

# The normal cadence is 25 seconds. This crosses it, so the first refusal lands
# and the board must keep the predictions it already has: a stale prediction
# carrying an honest age beats no prediction at all, and the age is on screen.
wait 30s

# A first failure waits a minute. Thirty seconds is not a minute, so nothing is
# asked here at all -- which is the point of the frame. `due_in` counts down
# rather than resetting, and the age keeps climbing.
wait 30s

# Now the minute is up. The second refusal doubles the wait to two minutes.
wait 40s

# Not due yet: the backoff really did grow.
wait 60s

# And recovery. A fresh publication, and the cadence returns to 25 seconds
# rather than staying backed off. Each attempt is recorded at the moment it came
# due, not at the moment this script noticed, so the counts below are the ones a
# running app would reach.
wait 50s

# A wait that spans several intervals at the recovered 25-second cadence. The
# app would have polled at each of them, so three answers are taken, not one.
# Serving a single answer per step made this read as one attempt and left
# `due_in` back at 25 as though the cadence had been kept.
wait 90s

# The refusal above, taken after a recovery. The wait that follows must start at
# a minute. A retained backoff would make it four.
wait 30s

# Nothing left in the queue. The cadence comes round, finds no answer, and the
# attempt stays owed: `due_in` holds at zero and the request count stops. That
# is what a feed gone quiet looks like from inside, and a port that polls more
# often than this one arrives here sooner.
wait 60s

# Long enough for the first bus to go. A board loaded once keeps the departure
# that has already left unless something re-queries it, and every other fixture
# holds its clock still, so nothing was asking. The 08:26 row must be gone by
# the time this frame is drawn.
wait 1600s
